      "observation": "{\"text\":\"Skip to main content Social Media Links Search Header Navigation News Opinion More topics Politics Sports Entertainment Education Lifestyle #NigeriaDecides Main navigation Videos Photos Documents Air Strike Hits Saudi Arabia’s Sovereign Wealth Fund, Second-Tallest Skyscraper November 19, 2026 News The 80-storey skyscraper, which houses the headquarters of the kingdom’s sovereign wealth fund, sustained damage to its facade, with several windows reportedly blown out. Saudi Arabia’s Public Investment Fund (PIF) Tower in Riyadh was damaged on Thursday following a missile attack blamed on Yemen’s Houthi rebels, as the kingdom reported a fresh wave of strikes targeting its capital and southern region. The 80-storey skyscraper, which houses the headquarters of the kingdom’s sovereign wealth fund, sustained damage to its facade, with several windows reportedly blown out. Witnesses told Agence France-Presse (AFP) that the damage affected several floors of the building, which stands 385 metres tall in Riyadh’s King Abdullah Financial District. The Saudi-led military coalition fighting the Houthis said it had intercepted a missile threat launched towards Riyadh. Coalition spokesman Turki al-Malki said an initial assessment indicated that debris from the interception had caused material damage, adding that authorities were examining the impact. Earlier on Thursday, AFP journalists in the Saudi capital reported hearing a powerful explosion. Saudi civil defence authorities subsequently issued a warning of potential danger before announcing minutes later that the threat had passed. The PIF Tower is one of the most prominent buildings in Riyadh’s financial district, which also houses the headquarters of the Saudi stock exchange and several multinational companies, including Goldman Sachs. The sovereign wealth fund, chaired by Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, manages more than $900bn in assets and plays a central role in Saudi Arabia’s efforts to diversify its economy away from oil. The latest attacks have raised concerns over the kingdom’s economic ambitions as fighting intensifies in neighbouring Yemen. The coalition also said it intercepted five ballistic missiles launched towards Saudi Arabia’s southern region, which borders Yemen. The Iran-backed Houthis have intensified attacks against Saudi targets in recent weeks, including airports, energy infrastructure, military installations and financial facilities. In October, an attack on Riyadh’s King Khalid International Airport killed 14 people and injured hundreds, forcing the suspension of flights for two weeks. In response, the Saudi-led coalition launched strikes against Houthi positions in areas under the group’s control in Yemen. On Thursday, Houthi-affiliated Almasirah television reported another Saudi attack on the Yemeni capital, Sanaa, alleging that forces targeted a shoe and textile factory belonging to the Alyateem Development Foundation. However, the Saudi-led coalition said it had destroyed a ballistic missile launch platform in Dhamar, about 100 kilometres south of Sanaa, which it said was being used by the Houthis to launch attacks towards Riyadh. The renewed fighting follows the collapse of a ceasefire that had largely contained Yemen’s long-running civil war since 2022. The Houthis have made significant territorial gains since hostilities resumed this summer and are reportedly advancing towards Aden, the interim capital of Yemen’s internationally recognised government. According to the United Nations, the latest fighting has killed more than 1,600 people and displaced over 300,000 others. Meanwhile, Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman met with the head of the United States Central Command, Admiral Brad Cooper, in Riyadh on Thursday. Saudi state news agency SPA reported that both sides discussed bilateral relations, particularly military cooperation, and developments in the region. The meeting followed another explosion in Riyadh on Wednesday, 